.\" Manual page for the jf command.
.\" Render it while you edit with: man ./docs/man/jf.1
.TH JF 1 "2026-08-25" "jackfield" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
jf \- the patchbay for agent credentials
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jf
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]
.br
.B jf help
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.br
.B jf \-\-version
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jf
answers two questions.
Which identity does a command line tool get in this directory?
And where does that credential come from?
.PP
A manifest,
.IR jackfield.yaml ,
answers the first question.
It maps a directory to a workspace, and a workspace to the profiles that each
command may use.
.B jf run
starts the tool under one of those profiles.
It removes the ambient credential variables, and it rejects the arguments that
would select another account, so the child process cannot change identity.
.PP
The jackfield hub answers the second question.
The hub holds the credentials themselves.
This machine is a cache.
.B jf login
signs this machine in to the hub,
.B jf status
shows where every connection stands, and
.B jf creds get
reads one credential for a script.
.PP
A machine can use either half alone.
A machine that only reaches the hub needs no manifest.
A machine that only gates local tools needs no hub.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Workspace commands
.TP
.BI "run [\-\-profile " NAME "] " COMMAND " [" ARGS .\|.\|.]
Run a command line tool under the identity of this directory.
.B jf
finds the workspace that owns the working directory, picks the profile that this
workspace allows for that command, and starts the tool.
A workspace with more than one allowed profile and no default needs
.BR \-\-profile .
.TP
.BI "resolve [\-\-profile " NAME "] " COMMAND " [" ARGS .\|.\|.]
Show which identity a command would get, and run nothing.
It prints the workspace, the command, the profile, and the executable.
It starts no child process, so it is safe to run at any time.
.
.SS Hub commands
.TP
.BI "login [\-name " NAME "] [\-device\-code | \-browser]"
Sign this machine in to the hub.
.B jf
prints a short code and a URL, then waits while you approve the machine in a
browser.
It stores the device token in
.IR ~/.config/jackfield/device\-token .
.RS
.PP
.B jf
picks the flow when you give no flag.
A machine reached over SSH, or a Linux machine with no graphical session, gets
the device-code flow.
That is a guess about the environment, so
.B \-device\-code
and
.B \-browser
override it.
.RE
.TP
.B devices
List the machines that hold a device token, and mark the one you are on.
.TP
.BI "devices revoke " NAME
Revoke one machine's token, by the name that the list shows or by its device id.
Any machine can revoke any other, so you can revoke a lost laptop from the
machine still in your hand.
Revoking this machine is allowed, and
.B jf
says so, because the next hub command here then needs
.B jf login
again.
.TP
.B status
Show where every connection stands.
The panel shows the hub address, the name of this machine, and one line per
connection: its identity, the age of its credential, and whether the upstream
service still accepts it.
.TP
.BI "auth [\-identity " WHO "] [\-stdin] [\-ticket " TICKET "] " CONNECTION
Store a credential in the hub.
A write needs a fresh browser approval every time, so
.B jf
opens the hub's approval page and asks you to paste back the ticket the page
shows.
.B jf
reads the secret from a hidden prompt, or from standard input with
.BR \-stdin ,
and never from a command argument.
Arguments appear in the process list, where any other process on the machine
reads them.
.TP
.BI "creds get [\-no\-cache] " CONNECTION
Read one credential from the hub.
The value goes to standard output alone, so a script reads it with a command
substitution.
Every other message goes to standard error.
.
.SS Other commands
.TP
.B version
Print the version of
.BR jf .
A release build prints its tag.
A build from source prints
.BR dev .
.TP
.BI "help [" COMMAND ]
Print the overview of every command, or the help for one command.
.B "jf " COMMAND " \-h"
prints the same page.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Read this manifest instead of searching for
.IR jackfield.yaml .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the overview and exit.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I jackfield.yaml
The manifest.
It maps directories to workspaces, workspaces to the commands they allow, and
commands to the profiles they may use.
It also holds the
.B hub:
key, the normal place for the hub address.
.B jf
searches for it in the working directory, then in each parent directory, then in
.IR ~/.config/jackfield/jackfield.yaml .
.TP
.I ~/.config/jackfield/device\-token
This machine's device token, written by
.BR "jf login" .
The file has mode 0600 and its directory has mode 0700.
.B jf
refuses to read a token file that other users can read, because a leaked device
token reads every credential in the hub.
.RS
.PP
This is a file, not a keychain item, on purpose.
A headless machine has no unlocked login session, so a keychain item is
unreadable there.
.RE
.TP
.I ~/.cache/jackfield/
The cached credentials, with mode 0600 on each file and mode 0700 on the
directory.
An entry lives for five minutes.
That is short enough that a credential
.B jf auth
replaced reaches every machine within five minutes, and long enough that a shell
loop does not open a connection for every call.
.TP
.I ~/.local/jackfield/bin/
The optional command shims.
They make
.BR gog ,
.BR wrangler ,
and
.B aws
run through the gate.
Put this directory first on
.BR PATH .
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B JF_CONFIG
The manifest to read.
It overrides the search, and
.B \-\-config
overrides it.
.TP
.B JF_HUB
The hub address.
It overrides the
.B hub:
key of the manifest.
Use it to reach a second deployment without editing the file that every machine
shares.
.TP
.B JF_TOKEN_FILE
The device token file, instead of
.IR ~/.config/jackfield/device\-token .
.TP
.B JF_CACHE_DIR
The credential cache directory, instead of
.IR ~/.cache/jackfield/ .
.
.SH EXAMPLES
See which Google account
.B gog
gets in this directory, and run nothing:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jf resolve gog
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a tool under the identity of this workspace:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jf run gog whoami \-\-plain
jf run wrangler r2 bucket list
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pick the profile when a workspace allows more than one.
AWS has no default on purpose, because staging and production carry different
risk:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jf run \-\-profile aws\-smarta\-staging aws sts get\-caller\-identity
.EE
.RE
.PP
With the shims on
.BR PATH ,
the same commands are shorter:
.PP
.RS
.EX
gog whoami \-\-plain
wrangler r2 bucket list
aws \-\-jf\-profile aws\-smarta\-staging sts get\-caller\-identity
.EE
.RE
.PP
Sign a machine in to the hub, and name it in the device list:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jf login \-name macbook
jf login \-device\-code
.EE
.RE
.PP
See where every connection stands, then list and revoke machines:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jf status
jf devices
jf devices revoke grumpyorange
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read one credential in a script:
.PP
.RS
.EX
token=$(jf creds get slack\-smarta)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Store a credential:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jf auth slack\-smarta
jf auth \-identity you@example.com slack\-smarta
printf '%s' "$SECRET" | jf auth \-stdin \-ticket TICKET slack\-smarta
.EE
.RE
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B jf
exits 0 when the command succeeds, and 1 when it fails.
.B jf run
exits with the status of the child process, so a script reads the tool's own
result.
.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B this machine has no device token; run `jf login`
No hub command works until this machine is signed in.
Run
.BR "jf login" .
.TP
.B no hub address
.B jf
found no hub to talk to.
Add a
.B hub:
key to
.IR jackfield.yaml ,
or set
.BR JF_HUB .
.TP
.B the working directory ... is in no workspace of this manifest
The manifest covers no workspace whose
.B roots:
contain this directory.
Add one, or change to a directory that a workspace already covers.
.TP
.B this command has more than one allowed profile and no default
Name a profile with
.BR \-\-profile .
The message lists the profiles that this workspace allows.
.
